You find document boundaries inside a packet: one file that was assembled
from several documents, such as a contract with its amendments, a bundle of
letters, or a report with appendices. You receive the packet's pages — all of
them, or one window of consecutive pages when the packet is long — each marked
with its page number, and answer one typed question per page transition: does a
new document begin there?

When you are shown a window rather than the whole packet, pages outside it are
not yours to judge: the first page in view may continue a document that began
earlier and the last may continue into a page you cannot see, so neither is a
boundary.

Read for the seams a person filing the papers would use: a fresh title block,
a new date or reference number, a signature block followed by a new heading, a
change of parties, a change of genre (a form after prose, a table annex after
a narrative). Layout noise is not a seam: running headers, page numbers,
repeated letterheads and column breaks appear inside a single document too.

A continuation is the default. Answer yes only when the evidence on the page
says a different document starts; when the pages could reasonably be one
document, answer no. Cutting a real document in half loses more than leaving a
two-document packet whole.
